oblem more efficiently.\n\nHowever, if you prefer a quicker method, you can use the standard algorithm for multiplication. Here's how it works:\n\n1. Write the numbers one below the other, aligning the digits by place value.\n2. Multiply 560 by 0 (the units digit of 500), which gives 0.\n3. Multiply 560 by 50 (the tens digit of 500), which gives 28000.\n4. Add the results: 0 + 28000 = 28000.\n\nWhile the breakdown method is more intuitive and easier to understand, the standard algorithm is quicker and more straightforward. Both methods will give you the correct answer of 280,000.\n</answer>.<|User|>Could you provide an example of how to use the standard algorithm for this problem? I want to make sure I understand it fully.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>.\nCertainly! Let's walk through the standard algorithm for multiplying 560 by 500 step by step:\n\n1. Write the numbers one below the other, aligning the digits by place value:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ```\n\n2. Since 500 has two zeros, we can think of it as 5 tens (5 x 10) and place two zeros at the end of the product. This makes the multiplication easier to visualize:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ----\n   ```\n\n3. Now, we'll multiply 560 by 5 (the tens digit of 500):\n   - First, multiply 560 by 5:\n     ```\n     560\n   x   5\n   ----\n     2800\n     ```\n   - This gives us 2800. Since we're actually multiplying by 500 (which is 5 tens), we need to shift the result one place to the left (because we're multiplying by 100) and add two zeros at the end:\n     ```\n     2800\n   ----\n     28000\n     ```\n\n4. Now, add the results:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ----\n     28000\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of 560 and 500 is 280,000.\n\nTo summarize, the standard algorithm involves:\n- Multiplying the numbers as if they were regular numbers (560 x 5 = 2800).\n- Shifting the result one place to the left for each zero in the multiplier (500 has two zeros, so shift left twice).\n- Adding the results.\n\nThis method is efficient and works well for larger numbers, but it requires careful attention to place value and the placement of zeros.\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nCertainly! Let's go through the standard algorithm step by step for multiplying 560 by 500:\n\n1. Write the numbers one below the other, aligning the digits by place value:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ----\n   ```\n\n2. Since 500 has two zeros, we can think of it as 5 tens (5 x 10) and place two zeros at the end of the product. This makes the multiplication easier to visualize:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ----\n   ```\n\n3. Now, we'll multiply 560 by 5 (the tens digit of 500):\n   - First, multiply 560 by 5:\n     ```\n     560\n   x   5\n   ----\n     2800\n     ```\n   - This gives us 2800. Since we're actually multiplying by 500 (which is 5 tens), we need to shift the result one place to the left (because we're multiplying by 100) and add two zeros at the end:\n     ```\n     2800\n   ----\n     28000\n     ```\n\n4. Now, add the results:\n   ```\n     560\n   x 500\n   ----\n     28000\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of 560 and 500 is 280,000.\n\nTo summarize, the standard algorithm involves:\n- Multiplying the numbers as if they were regular numbers (560 x 5 = 2800).\n- Shifting the result one place to the left for each zero in the multiplier (500 has two zeros, so shift left twice).\n- Adding the results.\n\nThis method is efficient and works well for larger numbers, but it requires careful attention to place value and the placement of zeros.\n</answer>.<|User|>Could you provide an example of how to use the distributive property to solve this problem? I want to see how it works in practice.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>.\nCertainly! The distributive property of multiplication over addition allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. In this case, we can break down 560 and 500 into their simpler forms and then use the distributive property to solve the problem.\n\nLet's break down the numbers:\n- 560 can be thought of as 56 tens (56 x 10).\n- 500 can be thought of as 5 hundreds (5 x 100).\n\nUsing the distributive property, we can rewrite the multiplication as:\n\\[ 560 \\times 500 = (56 \\times 10) \\times (5 \\times 100) \\]\n\nNow, let's solve this step by step:\n\n1. First, multiply 56 by 10:\n   \\[ 56 \\times 10 = 560 \\]\n\n2. Next, multiply 5 by 100:\n   \\[ 5 \\times 100 = 500 \\]\n\n3. Now, multiply the results from steps 1 and 2:\n   \\[ 560 \\times 500 = (56 \\times 10) \\times (5 \\times 100) = 560 \\times 500 = 560 \\times (5 \\times 100) = 560 \\times 5 \\times 100 \\]\n\n4. Finally, multiply 560 by 5:\n   \\[ 560 \\times 5 = 2800 \\]\n\n5. Then, multiply the result by 100:\n   \\[ 2800 \\times 100 = 280000 \\]\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get the same result:\n\\[ 560 \\times 500 = 280000 \\]\n\nThis method breaks down the problem into simpler steps and uses the distributive property to make the multiplication easier to manage.\n</think>.\n<answer>.\nCertainly! Let's use the distributive property to solve the problem of finding the product of 560 and 500 step by step:\n\n1. Break down the numbers using the distributive property:\n   \\[ 560 \\times 500 = (56 \\times 10) \\times (5 \\times 100) \\]\n\n2. First, multiply 56 by 10:\n   \\[ 56 \\times 10 = 560 \\]\n\n3. Next, multiply 5 by 100:\n   \\[ 5 \\times 100 = 500 \\]\n\n4. Now, multiply the results from steps 2 and 3:\n   \\[ (56 \\times 10) \\times (5 \\times 100) = 560 \\times 500 = 560 \\times (5 \\times 100) = 560 \\times 5 \\times 100 \\]\n\n5. Finally, multiply 560 by 5:\n   \\[ 560 \\times 5 = 2800 \\]\n\n6. Then, multiply the result by 100:\n   \\[ 2800 \\times 100 = 280000 \\]\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get:\n\\[ 560 \\times 500 = 280000 \\]\n\nThis method breaks down the problem into simpler steps and uses the distributive property to make the multiplication easier to manage.\n</answer>.<|User|>Could you provide an example of how 