f the product, we can perform the following divisions:\n- Dividing the product 195,300 by 930 gives us 210, which is one of the original numbers.\n- Dividing the product 195,300 by 210 gives us 930, which is the other original number.\nSince both divisions yield the correct original numbers, this confirms that the product 195,300 is indeed correct.</answer>.<|User|>That's very helpful. Could you also explain how to use the distributive property to break down the multiplication of 930 and 210 into simpler parts?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\n<think>.\nCertainly! The distributive property of multiplication over addition allows us to break down the multiplication of 930 and 210 into simpler parts. Here's how we can do it step by step:\n\n1. **Express 930 and 210 using the distributive property:**\n   - We can write 930 as \\(900 + 30\\) and 210 as \\(200 + 10\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(930 \\times 210 = (900 + 30) \\times (200 + 10)\\).\n\n2. **Apply the distributive property:**\n   - The distributive property states that \\(a \\times (b + c) = a \\times b + a \\times c\\).\n   - Applying this property, we get:\n     \\[\n     (900 + 30) \\times (200 + 10) = (900 \\times 200) + (900 \\times 10) + (30 \\times 200) + (30 \\times 10)\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate each term separately:**\n   - \\(900 \\times 200 = 180,000\\)\n   - \\(900 \\times 10 = 9,000\\)\n   - \\(30 \\times 200 = 6,000\\)\n   - \\(30 \\times 10 = 300\\)\n\n4. **Add all the terms together:**\n   - \\(180,000 + 9,000 + 6,000 + 300 = 195,300\\)\n\nSo, by using the distributive property, we can break down the multiplication of 930 and 210 into simpler parts and then add the results to get the final product, which is 195,300.\n</think>.\n<answer>. To use the distributive property to break down the multiplication of 930 and 210, we can express 930 as \\(900 + 30\\) and 210 as \\(200 + 10\\). Then, we apply the distributive property as follows:\n\\[\n(900 + 30) \\times (200 + 10) = (900 \\times 200) + (900 \\times 10